The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a> unsurprisingly offers performance similar to the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a>, according to an <a href="https://browser.geekbench.com/v7/cpu/630904">early iPhone Duo benchmark</a> shared on Geekbench 7. Both devices are equipped with Apple's 2nm A20 Pro chip with a 6-core CPU and a 7-core GPU.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iphone-duo-feature.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068262" /><br/>
The benchmarked &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; (iPhone19,4) is running iOS 27.1, as expected, and it had a single-core score of 3986 and a multi-core score of 11067. That's in line with multiple benchmarks collected from &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; and &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max models.<br/>
<br/>
Scores vary from test to test, so while the result does fall short of the top &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; benchmark scores, we're only viewing a single score. The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; has the same 6-core CPU with a 4.93GHz base frequency that's identified in &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; benchmarks, and it lists 11.30GB memory, which means 12GB of RAM.<br/>
<br/>
While the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; and &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; models use the same A20 Pro chip, there could be differences in real-world performance based on thermal management, the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;'s two screens vs. a single screen, and other factors.<br/>
<br/>
Apple says the A20 Pro offers up to 20 percent faster CPU performance than the A19 Pro and up to 40 percent faster GPU performance. GPU speeds were not tested.<br/>
